  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Lagrange Multipliers: Regularization as Constrained Optimization** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef calculus_lagrange_regularization(X: list, y: list, l2_lambda: float = 0.5) -> dict:\n    Xa, ya = np.array(X, dtype=np.float64), np.array(y, dtype=np.float64)\n    w = np.dot(np.linalg.inv(np.dot(Xa.T, Xa) + float(l2_lambda)*np.eye(Xa.shape[1])), np.dot(Xa.T, ya))\n    return {'regularized_weights': np.round(w, 4).tolist(), 'weight_norm': round(float(np.linalg.norm(w)), 4)}\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
